On the Trajectories of Affection

It's a rough game anyone knows
There are no rules, anything goes
There's no logical explanation
For this discombooberation
It's a most bemuddling, 
most befuddling thing 

There's no sensible explanation 
For this discombooberation
It's a most hodge-podgical, 
most illogical, 
most confusiling, 
most bamboozling, 
most bemuddling, 
most befuddling thing!

-from Disney’s “The Sword in the Stone”, Composed by Richard M. Sherman and Robert B. Sherman

Peculiar, indeed. That it can strike first thing, right out of the gate.  Or, sometimes, after a conversation, sometimes several conversations, or sometimes building in increments over the span of several years. One can be compelled, the other repelled, only to switch mid-way through the proceedings, barely hovering above some mysterious anchor - as though each has embedded within them the laws of the Tango. It can be forged with a passing friendship, or with a sudden pang. Or, more unusually, some nagging dislike - bearing with it a kind of schoolchild’s determination to bully and tease, to pick on, to pull a finger or pretend not to notice. That then blossoms into a lifelong journey. Or collapses mid-air. One can be overwhelmed, the other at-ease. One can be ready to plunge, the other skeptical. One is certain to depart, only to reverse-course, and find that the other has long left. There are inklings, which are sometimes more like gargantuan billboards in one’s consciousness, about the nature of a relationship that is about to commence. The more “in tune” with oneself - the more accurate the inkling. And yet. There is so much richness, so many seemingly singular, irreproducible moments between two people, that no one can foresee, or ever accurately represent with reductive “I had a similar thing” insights, with words and other symbols, though “objective” parties cannot help but try, as in such a case, to patch together the unpredictable trajectories of affection.

~HS
